added_lemmas.statement:
There is a constant \(c_{\beta,D}>0\), depending only on \(\beta,D\), such that for every admissible \(n,m,\tau\),
\[
\mathcal R(n,m,\tau)\ge c_{\beta,D}\min\!\left\{\ell,\;
\ell\min\{1,N^{-\varkappa}\}
\left[1+\min\!\left\{
\left(\ell\min\{1,N^{-\varkappa}\}\right)^{\varkappa/(1-\varkappa)},
[\log(2m)]^{\varkappa}
\right\}\right]\right\}.                                      \tag{21}
\]
The bound is witnessed by an explicit product prior on paired Hall splices, all of whose full-data laws belong to \(\mathcal H_{\beta,D}(m)\), and it holds against every measurable randomized learner in \(\mathfrak A_{n,m}\).  It retains the exact logarithmic mixture loss after arbitrary capacity reallocation and strengthens the constant-information lower bound in the nonvanishing intermediate and familywise-testing regimes.

obligation.partial_result:
Put \(A_{n,m,\tau}:=\ell\min\{1,N^{-\varkappa}\}\) and \(L_m:=\log(2m)\).  In addition to the proved generic upper bound, the paired-splice product prior now yields the sharper same-class lower envelope
\[
\mathcal R(n,m,\tau)\geq c_{\beta,D}\min\!\left\{\ell,\;
A_{n,m,\tau}\left[1+\min\!\left\{
A_{n,m,\tau}^{\varkappa/(1-\varkappa)},L_m^{\varkappa}
\right\}\right]\right\}.
\]
For every splice amplitude, conditioning on the multinomial pair counts preserves posterior independence.  A concavity exchange proves that arbitrary zero/double capacity reallocations cannot beat one posterior-MAP action per pair for the exact-loss lower envelope.  At least \(m/4\) pairs have count at most \(4N\), and their MAP errors dominate independent Bernoulli variables with probability \(c\exp\{-C N\delta^{2+4/\beta}\}\).  This gives an explicit binomial lower bound for the exact missed-cell identity from constant through order-\(\log m\) testing information.  The optimized lower envelope has the constant-information scale \(A_{n,m,\tau}\), the intermediate scale \(A_{n,m,\tau}^{1/(1-\varkappa)}\), and the familywise scale \(A_{n,m,\tau}L_m^{\varkappa}=\mathcal B_{\mathrm{CK}}(n,m,\tau)\), subject to the global cap \(\ell\).  The proved full-class upper bound remains \(C_{\beta,D}\mathcal B_{\mathrm{CK}}(n,m,\tau)\); no matching capacity-aware upper bound is yet established.
